    answer: 'A cell phone plan is deductible only for its business-use share. Section 162 allows ordinary and necessary business costs and section 262 disallows the personal part, so one phone used for both needs a reasonable, documented split, for example business calls and data over a representative month. A second line or device used only for the business is fully a business cost. A client reimbursement removes the deduction for the reimbursed part. The base charge for the first landline into your home is personal by statute even if you use it for work; only business long-distance charges and a dedicated second line qualify. Cell phones are no longer "listed property", so a log of every call is not required, but the percentage must rest on something you can show. The handset itself is a purchase rather than a bill, and an expensive phone follows the equipment rules.',
