    title: 'IRS Publication 587 (2025): Utilities and services in a home used for business',
    url: 'https://www.irs.gov/publications/p587',
    reviewedAt, supportedTaxYears,
    summary: 'Home internet or broadband bills. The business-use share is a utility deducted directly on Schedule C, separate from the home office deduction.',
    requiredFacts: ['What share of your home internet use is business, and how did you estimate it?', 'Do you also claim a home office, and which method do you use?'],
    answer: 'Home internet is deductible for its business-use share, and it stays out of the home office computation. Deduct the business percentage of the bill directly as a utility; do not run it through Form 8829 or the simplified method, which covers only the space itself. The percentage should reflect actual use: hours or data for client work, uploads, video calls and research versus streaming and household use. A separate connection used only for the business is deductible in full, subject to your records. Without a home office the business share can still qualify because the internet cost stands on its own under section 162, but the personal portion is never deductible. Bundled cable, streaming or phone add-ons are personal unless used in the business.',
