    answer: 'Travel expenses are deductible when you are away from your tax home, the general area of your main place of business, substantially longer than a normal workday and need sleep or rest to meet the demands of the work. Transportation, lodging, baggage, taxis and business calls qualify in full when the trip is primarily business. Meals while traveling are 50% deductible, using either actual cost or the federal per-diem meal rate for the location; lodging must be actual. Personal days are separated: if the trip is primarily personal, the airfare is personal and only costs directly tied to business activity qualify. Weekend days between business meetings can count when staying is cheaper than returning. Bringing a spouse or family adds no deduction unless they are employees with a business reason. Travel to a temporary work location outside your metropolitan area is different from commuting. Records are strict: no deduction is allowed without dates, place, purpose and amounts.',
