    title: 'IRS Form 7206 instructions (2025): Self-employed health insurance deduction',
    url: 'https://www.irs.gov/instructions/i7206',
    reviewedAt, supportedTaxYears,
    summary: 'Health insurance premiums for the self-employed person and family, including Medicare and marketplace plans. A Schedule 1 adjustment through Form 7206, not a Schedule C expense.',
    requiredFacts: ['Were you or your spouse eligible for an employer health plan in any month this year?', 'Is the policy for you and your family, or coverage you provide to employees?'],
    answer: 'Health insurance premiums for you, your spouse, dependents and children under 27 are deductible for a self-employed person, but not on Schedule C. Section 162(l) makes them an adjustment to income on Schedule 1 through Form 7206, so they reduce income tax but not self-employment tax, and they reduce your qualified business income. Medicare premiums count. The deduction is capped at the net profit of the business minus half of self-employment tax and any self-employed retirement deduction, and it cannot create a loss. You lose it for any month you were eligible for an employer-subsidized plan through your own W-2 job or a spouse\'s employer, even if you did not enroll. Marketplace premiums covered by the premium tax credit are deductible only for the part you actually paid. Premiums for coverage you provide to employees are a Schedule C expense.',
