    answer: 'Dental and vision costs split into two questions. Premiums for dental and vision insurance are part of the self-employed health insurance deduction on Schedule 1 (Form 7206), subject to the same net-profit and employer-plan rules as medical premiums. Out-of-pocket costs such as cleanings, fillings, braces, eye exams, glasses and contacts are personal medical expenses under section 213, deductible only if you itemize and only above 7.5% of adjusted gross income. They are never Schedule C expenses, even for a photographer who needs sharp eyesight. Safety glasses or prescription protective eyewear required for a job site can be a business supply. Paying these bills through a health savings account is another tax-favored route. Coverage you provide to employees is a Schedule C expense.',
