    answer: 'Bartering is taxable. When you trade your services or products for someone else\'s, the fair market value of what you receive is business income in the year you receive it, reported on Schedule C just as if you had been paid cash, and it is subject to self-employment tax. If the traded item or service would have been a deductible business expense had you paid for it, you can deduct its value as an expense, so a trade of design work for bookkeeping produces both income and an expense. A trade for something personal, such as a vacation or a haircut, produces income with no deduction. Barter exchanges report trades on Form 1099-B. Both sides should agree on the value in writing at the time of the trade. Casual one-off swaps between friends outside your business are not business income.',
